<script module>
  export { noscript, intro, feedback, roleModel, how, hue, warp, spread, hueShare, hueSlots, selection, schemeWheel, lightness, rampControls, fade, saturation, gamut, palette, paletteSpace, demoPostExcerpt, creator, module, credits }
</script>

<!-- Page -->

{#snippet noscript()}

Color Horse needs JavaScript to generate palettes. Please turn it on to use the app.

{/snippet}

<!-- Control panel -->

{#snippet intro()}

Color Horse uses modern color science to generate a harmonious color palette from any two colors.

{/snippet}

{#snippet feedback()}

Have feedback, or found a bug? Send a message to [@color.horse](https://bsky.app/profile/color.horse) on Bluesky.

{/snippet}

{#snippet roleModel()}

This palette was made with an earlier version of Color Horse, so its roles stay as they were. Update to keep Success, Info, Warning, and Danger in their own color families.

{/snippet}

<!-- Theory tab, in reading order -->

{#snippet how()}

Color Horse uses math to create pleasing color palettes from any two colors.

How?

Color Horse breaks the two colors into three properties:
- Hue
- Lightness
- Saturation

If we treat those three numbers like coordinates in 3D space, then all we need to do is evenly distribute our remaining colors around the first two. That's where the math comes in.

{/snippet}

{#snippet hue()}

Classic color theory describes a handful of conventional color harmonies, like complementary (opposites) and analogous (neighbors). These conventions are usually described as fixed positions on a color wheel. But, there's something most people don't know: the color wheel itself is subjective. In fact, there is no one definitive color wheel. So, rather than changing the position of colors on the wheel, we can change the shape of the wheel itself.

{/snippet}

{#snippet warp()}

When you choose two colors, Color Horse warps the standard color wheel to fit your two colors. Then it chooses another ten colors with a smooth distribution around the wheel.

{/snippet}

{#snippet spread()}

You might want your color wheel to be more yellow or more blue. To shift the emphasis of the wheel, you can change the spread between your two anchor colors.

{/snippet}

{#snippet hueShare()}

The twelve points that Color Horse chooses become the twelve hues in your palette.

{/snippet}

{#snippet hueSlots()}

When you distribute those twelve hues evenly, you get a new color wheel. On this wheel, Color Horse identifies semantic roles, like "Accent" and "Warning".

{/snippet}

{#snippet selection()}

With a standard spread, Color Horse can reliably create an eight-color scheme:
- Primary
- Secondary
- Tertiary
- Accent
- Info
- Success
- Warning
- Danger

With a non-standard spread, the resulting wheel might not be able to accommodate certain roles, so Color Horse places as many as it can.

{/snippet}

{#snippet schemeWheel()}

Once you pick out the hues that have specific roles, you have a completely unique color scheme.

{/snippet}

{#snippet lightness(svg)}

While hue sits on a circle, lightness sits on a line — from black to white. Color Horse takes the lightness of your two input colors and places them on the line. Then, it places the remaining shades around them to create a "ramp."

{/snippet}

{#snippet rampControls()}

You can change the position of all of the shades in the ramp by adjusting the darkest shade, lightest shade, or contrast.

{/snippet}

{#snippet fade()}

Saturation is what makes a color go from beige to yellow or brown to red.

Typically, the lightest and darkest colors in a color palette will have less saturation. For each of your anchor colors, Color Horse computes a saturation curve.

{/snippet}

{#snippet saturation()}

Now, each shade-ramp is also a saturation-ramp. We can show lightness as height, and saturation as distance from the center of the color wheel, where the center is grey.

{/snippet}

{#snippet gamut()}

Colors appear differently on different surfaces. In particular, a computer screen can display more vibrant colors than a physical print. The range of available colors is called the "gamut." Color Horse provides an option to restrict the gamut for screen or print.

{/snippet}

{#snippet palette()}

With the ramps for your two anchor colors, Color Horse computes unique saturation values at each lightness for each hue, resulting in a full palette.

{/snippet}

{#snippet paletteSpace()}

To see the full palette as a 3D model, open the [Space](#space) tab.

{/snippet}

