{#snippet creator()}

Hi! My name is Sam. I'm the author of a static blog generator called [Vowel](https://vowel.cc). I wanted a way for Vowel users to create unique, beautiful color schemes for their websites. That's how I got the idea for Color Horse.

Do you have a cool project? I'm currently looking for my next role. I'm a technical writer and full-stack developer. Feel free to shoot me a message on [LinkedIn](https://linkedin.com/in/samlittlefair) or [Bluesky](https://bsky.app/profile/littlefair.ca).

{/snippet}

{#snippet module()}

Color Horse also exists as a zero-dependency npm package called `colorhorse`. Install it to generate color schemes in your own code.

```sh
npm install colorhorse
```

Pass two colors to `getScheme`. It returns each role's shades as hex colors, from darkest to lightest.

```js
import { getScheme } from "colorhorse";

getScheme("#3366cc", "#cc6633");
// { primary: ["#...", ...], secondary: [...], ... }
```

Options match the settings here: `wheel`, `gamut`, `shadeCount`, `minLightness`, `maxLightness`, `minChroma`, `contrast`, and `colorTwoSlot` for spread.

{/snippet}

{#snippet credits()}

This project is inspired by all of the creativity in the web dev community, especially developers working on color problems, like [Lea Verou](https://lea.verou.me/blog/2020/04/lch-colors-in-css-what-why-and-how/), [Adam Argyle](https://nerdy.dev/), and [David Aerne](https://bsky.app/profile/meodai.bsky.social). It's also worth mentioning that the branding of this website is not dissimilar to other [horse-themed design blogs](https://frontend.horse/).

The icons are from [Font Awesome](https://fontawesome.com), except for the horse icons, which are by [Lewen Design](https://thenounproject.com/creator/lewen.design) via Noun Project.

{/snippet}

